E\\equiv\\frac{1}{2}Mv_{0}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(M+\\frac{4\\pi}{3}f_{b}R^{3}\\rho \\right)v^{2}+2\\pi f_{b}R^{3}p. 

This approximation also works well during the Sedov-Taylor phase (Zeldovich & Raizer, 1966), because even at this stage most of the swept-up material is accumulated in a thin layer just downstream of the shock whereas the rest of the volume is filled by a rarefied, hot gas at nearly constant pressure. By eliminating p and introducing dimensionless variables:

